    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_57bcdc931c749033eb5baa5fd6d90734122bd2d2c2b80612aafbb8c6fd65cb6a",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The random-matrix analogue transports a two-directional scale dictionary, not merely a global statistic: radial displacement from the unit circle corresponds to abscissa displacement from the critical axis, angular shifts correspond to ordinate shifts, and matrix size n corresponds to log t. With these identifications the normalized log characteristic polynomial has the same min-type Gaussian covariance, while differences of its imaginary part give centered eigenvalue counts on arcs. Matching both perturbation directions is a stronger constraint than generic GUE resemblance.",
