    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e3a2f08c833c72a94ab196f5c23d9f52f24bd9b3b38b9910abd83f9ee2b092bb",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The ordered evaluation-point geometry in the zeta theorem supplies adjacent logarithmic separation exponents, with the covariance of two nonadjacent points equal to the weakest adjacent exponent between them, capped at one. Representing those exponents as heights in a rooted genealogy turns that min rule into shared Gaussian increments: two variables accumulate identical variance along their common ancestral path and independent variance only after their branches separate.",
