    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_fc6b469bdbfc2fee7de2c35af2b4b14382a93cd728ca3b46d775a4a1c5f4be11",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "In both the unitary and zeta calculations, normalized covariance is the logarithmic mass of modes that remain coherent before phase separation, damping, or the finite ambient cutoff intervenes. Matrix trace expansion gives a damped or undamped harmonic phase sum cut off at n, whereas the prime sum is completed through the zeta pole off the critical scale or converted by PNT and partial summation to the same logarithmic oscillatory integral at the critical scale. These distinct representations therefore yield the relative separation exponent capped at one.",
